  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA large upper level trough is currently located over the\nNortheast. The main upper level low is currently over eastern\nQuebec and it will be swinging southward towards New England for\ntoday. IR satellite imagery shows fairly widespread low stratus\nand stratocu clouds over the region and this looks to continue\nthrough the day today with the cyclonic flow and abundant low\nlevel moisture trapped beneath an inversion. The best chance of\nseeing some breaks of sun will be for across southern valley\nareas, mainly this afternoon.  \n\nAs the upper level low drops southward, some scattered rain and\nsnow showers will develop across the region today, thanks to the\ncyclonic flow in place. This will be especially try for upslope\nfavored areas (such as the Adirondacks and southern Greens).\nSome lake-moisture will help enhance precip as well, although\nthe north to northwest flow will generally favor areas west of\nour area across western and central NY for this. Highest\ncoverage of passing showers will be during the afternoon hours.\nSnow accumulation will be limited due to the brief nature of\nprecip and marginal temps, but a light coating to an inch cannot\nbe ruled out across the highest terrain of the Adirondacks and\nsouthern Greens today, with temps generally in the 30s through\nthe day. With the trough overhead, clouds will generally linger\ninto Saturday night with lows in the 20s to low 30s.\n\nThe upper level low will begin to open up and become more\nprogressive for Sunday as it starts to lift back out to the\neast. The upper level trough will be still in place to start the\nday, but will be departing as the day progresses. With the\nforcing moving away, most areas will stay dry on Sunday,\nalthough a few lingering upslope snow flurries are possible for\nthe Adirondacks. Otherwise, Sunday will be another cool day with\ntemps still in the 30s to low 40s. There will be more breaks of\nsun for Sunday by the afternoon, especially for valley areas,\nalthough the high terrain will probably still wind up being\nmostly cloudy for a good part of the day. \n\nDuring the upcoming week, upper level heights will be rising\nwith warming temps aloft. High pressure will be building towards\nthe area from the west, which will allow for a dry week. While\nthe week will start out with temps still on the cooler side,\nthey will be rising near to above normal by Tuesday or\nWednesday. Above normal temps are expected for the late week\nwith continued dry conditions and a partly to mostly sunny sky.\nThe next chance of passing showers may not be until next weekend\nor beyond.",
